  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Constant Signal",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a constant signal $x(t) = A into the transform pair X(j \\omega) = 2 pi A \\delta(\\omega) by recognizing constant time-domain content as a frequency-domain impulse at zero frequency$.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Constant Signal",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a Constant Signal",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A constant signal is a flat waveform with no time variation. In the Fourier transform, that kind of signal is read as pure DC content concentrated at $\\omega=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the unit-constant case first: because the signal never changes with time, all of its spectral weight lands at zero frequency. So the Fourier transform of $x(t)=1$ is the zero-frequency impulse pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once the unit case is clear, any constant amplitude $A$ just scales the spectrum by $A$. The impulse stays at $\\omega=0$; only its weight changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=2\\pi A\\delta(\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the pair in reverse as well: a zero-frequency impulse with weight $2\\pi A$ means the time-domain signal is the constant $A$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Constant Signal",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting the Unit Constant Signal to a Zero-Frequency Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=1$, choose the correct Fourier transform pair and identify the impulse at $\\omega=0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a unit constant signal: $x(t)=1$ is flat for all $t$, so it contains only DC content. That means the spectrum must collapse to the zero-frequency line.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the constant-signal rule from the tutorial: all spectral weight sits at $\\omega=0$, and the correct transform pair is a zero-frequency impulse with weight $2\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=2\\pi\\delta(\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "No other frequency content appears. The unit constant signal maps directly to $2\\pi\\delta(\\omega)$ centered at $\\omega=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Constant Signal",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Converting a Scaled Constant Signal to a Weighted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=A$, determine the Fourier transform and explain how the amplitude changes the impulse weight.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still a constant signal: $x(t)=A$ is flat for all $t$, so it carries only DC content. The new wrinkle is just the constant level $A$ instead of $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply linearity to the unit-constant result from the previous step: multiplying the time-domain signal by $A$ multiplies the spectrum by $A$ while leaving the impulse at $\\omega=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=2\\pi A\\delta(\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the weighted impulse pair is the same zero-frequency location as before, with the amplitude carried into the coefficient. The spectrum is still concentrated at $\\omega=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
